==Old English==

===Etymology===
Common to West and North {{etyl|gem|ang}}: cognate with {{etyl|non|-}} {{term|kǫttr|lang=non}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|katt|lang=sv}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{term|kazzo|lang=goh}}.  The word existed in the Germanic languages in a female gender also, represented in Old English by {{term|catte|lang=ang}}.  The word appears to be related to {{etyl|LL.|-}} {{term|cattus|lang=la}} as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain.

